      "writtenOn": "2026-10-07T04:21:02Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Josie’s point applies to the custom-TLD case: restoring the original `isValid()` implementation would bypass the configured validator.\n\nThis also preserves production behavior: `sendemail.allowTLD` is an operator setting. Once its overrides are instance-specific, validation must use that configured instance. The tests exercise that contract.\n\nFor example, with `sendemail.allowTLD \u003d example`, calling the Commons singleton directly would reject `foo@bar.example`, ignoring Gerrit’s configured allowance.\n\n`EmailValidatorIT.testCustomTopLevelDomain()` already covers this case.\n\nIn my follow-up change 639564, I’ve simplified construction by selecting the default or custom `DomainValidator` first, then creating one `EmailValidator` around it.",
